## 10. Appendix: Quick Reference

### Core Constructs (v2.0)
- `data` - Data models
- `interface` - UI sections
- `logic` - Business logic
- `computed` - Derived values
- `validate` - Validation rules
- `state_machine` - State management
- `realtime` - WebSocket connections
- `optimistic` - Optimistic updates
- `virtualized_list` - Large list rendering
- `external` - Third-party integrations
- `navigation` - Route guards

### APML-EDU Extensions (v2.1)
- `content` - Learning content hierarchies
- `methodology` - Pedagogical rules
- `production` - Runtime learning units
- `audio` - Audio pipeline configuration
- `parameters` - Scoped configuration
- `adaptation` - Dynamic adjustments

---

## 11. Compilation Roadmap

### 11.1 Current State (February 2026)

APML currently operates as **documentation-first specification** — human-readable files that
guide development across two repositories sharing a single Supabase database:

```
ssi-dashboard-v7-clean          ssi-learning-app-v2
(Content Creation)              (Content Delivery)
Node.js / Vue 3                 React 19 / Next.js 15
├── apml/ (20+ files)           ├── apml/ (20+ files)
└── services/                   └── packages/
    course-builder-api.cjs          @ssi/core (engine)
    production-api.cjs              @ssi/hooks
                                    @ssi/db
                    ┌─────────┐
                    │ Supabase│  ← Real contract
                    │   + S3  │
                    └─────────┘
```

**What APML is today:**
- Free-form markdown with YAML-like structure (NOT the formal grammar defined in Sections 2-8)
- Documentation that agents and humans read to understand the system
- Loosely typed — no compilation, no enforcement, no code generation
- Philosophical backbone via the 12 Directions (see `core/12-directions.apml`)

**What APML is not yet:**
- A compilable specification language
- A single source of truth that generates code
- Enforced by tooling at development time

This is intentional. The system is still evolving rapidly. Premature formalization would
constrain the organic discovery of what the learning engine needs.

### 11.2 Compilation Targets (Planned)

When the architecture stabilises, APML constructs will compile to concrete artifacts.

#### Tier 1 — Straightforward (Shared Contract Layer)

These constructs have clear, mechanical mappings:

| APML Construct | Compiles To | Consumers |
|----------------|-------------|-----------|
| `data` | TypeScript interfaces + Zod schemas + SQL migrations | Both repos |
| `content` | Typed entity definitions (Seed, LEGO, Phrase) | Both repos |
| `parameters` | Typed config objects with defaults | Both repos |
| Variable Registry | Shared enums (`CadenceType`, `LegoType`, `SampleStatus`) | Both repos |
| `validate` | Zod validators (shared via npm package) | Both repos |

**Why straightforward:** These are data shapes. The mapping from APML → TypeScript is
mechanical and deterministic. One source of truth, two consumers.

#### Tier 2 — Design Decisions Required

These constructs generate different code per framework:

| APML Construct | Dashboard (Vue 3) | Learning App (React 19) |
|----------------|-------------------|------------------------|
| `interface` | Vue SFCs + Pinia stores | React components + hooks |
| `state_machine` | Pinia state machines | @ssi/core CycleOrchestrator |
| `realtime` | Socket.IO composables | Supabase Realtime hooks |
| `optimistic` | Optimistic Pinia actions | React optimistic updates |
| `navigation` | Vue Router guards | Next.js middleware |

**Why challenging:** Same spec, two rendering targets. The APML compiler needs
framework-specific backends — like how LLVM has x86 and ARM backends.

#### Tier 3 — Research Required

These constructs encode domain expertise that is hard to formalize:

| APML Construct | Challenge |
|----------------|-----------|
| `methodology` | Pedagogical rules → @ssi/core engine logic |
| `production` | Runtime session assembly (basket ordering, spaced repetition) |
| `adaptation` | Dynamic difficulty adjustment algorithms |
| `audio` | TTS provider abstraction + S3 pipeline orchestration |

**Why research:** These encode the "how to teach" knowledge. The gap between spec
and implementation is semantic, not syntactic.

### 11.3 Architecture of the Compiler

```
apml/
├── compiler/
│   ├── parser.cjs              # APML → AST (exists, schema-only)
│   ├── backends/
│   │   ├── typescript.cjs      # AST → TS interfaces + Zod
│   │   ├── sql.cjs             # AST → Supabase migrations
│   │   ├── vue.cjs             # AST → Vue 3 SFCs (Tier 2)
│   │   └── react.cjs           # AST → React components (Tier 2)
│   └── registry/
│       └── variables.cjs       # Shared enum generation
```

**Compilation direction:** APML is the source of truth. Code is the output.

**Escape hatch (D09 Progressive Enhancement):** Any compiled output can be manually
extended. Generated files include `// APML-GENERATED — do not edit above this line`
markers, with a human-editable section below.

### 11.4 Strategic Approach

**Phase A — Shared Contract (Tier 1 only)**
- Compile `data` and `validate` constructs to a shared `@ssi/types` package
- Both repos import types from this package instead of defining their own
- Variable Registry becomes the canonical enum source
- **Trigger:** When ssi-learning-app-v2 reaches production stability

**Phase B — Framework Backends (Tier 2)**
- Build Vue 3 and React 19 compiler backends
- Generate scaffold code (not full components — that kills flexibility)
- Interface specs produce component contracts: props, events, slots/children
- **Trigger:** When both repos have stable component patterns to extract from

**Phase C — Domain Engine (Tier 3)**
- Formalise methodology and production constructs
- Compile to @ssi/core engine configuration
- This is the most valuable target — and the hardest to get right
- **Trigger:** When the learning methodology is proven across 5+ languages

### 11.5 Alignment with the 12 Directions

| Direction | How the Compilation Roadmap Aligns |
|-----------|-----------------------------------|
| D01 Parameterization | `parameters` construct compiles to typed config with defaults |
| D02 Separation of Concerns | Compiler backends separate spec from framework-specific output |
| D03 Immutability of Source | APML files are the immutable source; generated code is derived |
| D04 Idempotency | Compiling the same APML twice produces identical output |
| D05 Store Raw, Compute Derived | APML is raw; TS/Vue/React code is computed |
| D06 Explicit over Implicit | Compilation targets are declared, not inferred |
| D07 Context-Agnostic | Same APML compiles to Vue OR React via backend selection |
| D09 Progressive Enhancement | Escape hatches allow manual extension of generated code |
| D10 Observability | Generated code includes provenance comments (`// from: audio-registry.apml:42`) |
| D12 Composition | Compiler is composed of independent backends, each handling one target |

### 11.6 Current Stance

**Do not formalise prematurely.** The operational APML files are doing their job as
human-and-agent-readable specifications. The 12 Directions provide philosophical
coherence. The compilation roadmap above is the plan for when things stabilise.

The right moment to start compiling is when:
1. The same data shapes are manually synchronised across both repos (pain signal)
2. A type mismatch between repos causes a production bug (cost signal)
3. The learning methodology is stable enough to encode (maturity signal)

Until then, keep APML loose, keep it honest, and let the code teach us what the spec
should say.

